// find -- a name to a sprite id, and the transparency convention.
//
// THE TRANSPARENCY CONVENTION: a sprite pixel of -1 means "do not write".
//
// A packed colour is 0xRRGGBB, so every real colour is 0 .. 16777215 and -1
// cannot collide with one -- which is the whole reason to pick it rather than,
// say, magenta, the classic colour key that a game then cannot use. IDML_GAME.md
// section 2's `. clear` in a palette decodes to this value; nothing else in the
// engine treats any colour as special, and there is no alpha anywhere.
//
// It is a function rather than a literal so that every blit, the loader and the
// tests all name the same thing (NAMES.md section 5: slot numbers and reserved
// values are spoken by named zero-action functions, never as bare literals).
//
// Lookup is a linear scan, folded through the loop because there is no break.
// It runs at load time -- a name is resolved to an id once and the id is what
// the entity store carries -- so the cost is irrelevant and the code is three
// lines.

d2_clear() {
} return int 0 - 1;

d2_spr_find(string name) {
} return int d2_spr_scan(name, 0, 0 - 1);

// p walks the table; i carries the answer, -1 until a name matches. The `i < 0`
// guard makes the FIRST declaration win if a game declares one name twice (the
// loader reports that as an error, but this must not depend on it).
d2_spr_scan(string name, int p, int i) {
  while (p < len((import asset_sprn))) {
    if (((import asset_sprn)[p] = name) && (i < 0)) {
      i = p;
    }
    p = p + 1;
  }
} return int i;
